women were generally not studied in  clinical research trials until the  mid-1990s as a consequence of that there  was a serious lack of information on the  health of women in the 1990s things  started to change and they changed  largely because the National Institutes  of Health the single largest funder of  biomedical research in the country  change their requirement that women be  considered as subjects in clinical  research but research takes a long time  and it takes a long time to begin to  include women and to study women and to  learn and it really wasn't until about  the year 2000 and in the early 2000s  that we started to see the beginning of  new research coming out about women's  health and we're still only at the  beginning of understanding what we need  to know both about women's health and  about gender differences in health and  disease we started our program women's  health research at Yale in 1998 it began  with a very generous gift from private  foundation the Donahue foundation that  was also committed to Medical Health  Research women's health research at Yale  has four major component parts the first  is our pilot project program and that  program is designed to provide  investigators with pilot money so that  they can start innovative Studies on  women's health and on gender differences  gain the feasibility data they need to  go on and apply for larger grants and  answer more definitive questions about  the health of women the second part or  second component part of women's health  research at Yale is our involvement with  the community reaching out and  partnering with the community so that we  can provide the information that people  need to know about developing findings  in research the third major component of  our work is to build our own what we  call research cores and our research  cores are really dedicated to bringing  together scientists from different  disciplines  who will work together on a particular  large problem so for example our cores  focus around topic areas like  cardiovascular health women in trauma  and women in addiction all of these  areas really have been understudied and  really need our attention and the fourth  major area of our efforts the fourth  component part of women's health  research at Yale is training the next  generation we're very committed to  helping junior faculty postdoctoral  students undergraduate students begin to  learn about how to do research and most  specifically how to include gender in  that work  once we began the program and we saw the  tremendous interest on the part of  scientists we started talking to the  community as well and we saw that people  really reached out for information about  women's health women and men but  primarily women interested in women's  health and gender differences so we also  made a big commitment to communicate our  findings to the community and doing that  really not to just tell the community  what we knew but to partner with the  community ask the community what was  needed and how they needed the kind of  information from us that we had we're  interested in engaging the community in  a whole variety of ways we offer a  newsletter that goes out quarterly we  have a wonderful website and invite  people to our website we also are  involved now in social media so people  can contact us through Facebook or  Twitter but we want to reach out in  various ways that are comfortable and  familiar to people of all different ages  no matter where they are in the country  and even outside the country because our  reaches not only become national but has  begun to become international as well  the program is funded by the grants that  we receive as well as through an annual  appeal which really is tremendously  important to us in covering our  operating costs every year in addition  to that we're very interested in  building our endowment we have already  begun in that regard and are very  excited about seeing that as a source of  revenue over time so a program that  really directs our focus on women's  health and on gender differences exists  in perpetuity
 

